# Three patterns can hold an err: `(err …)`, an `:err` annotation, and a
# TYPESET with err among its members. Under the 2026-09-15 ruling a bare err
# is data and all three match it wherever it was raised — and the typeset is
# the one to pin separately, because it is the shape that diverged before:
# native's typeset arm returned before asking about the raiser while the
# other two engines asked, so a program answered differently by engine
# (kanso#1056). The raiser no longer matters, and every engine has to agree
# that it does not.
#
# `own` is this module's failure reaching this module's typeset arm: the arm
# takes it, and `when_failed`, handed a string, answers false. The value line
# is the other half: the arm does its job on a member that is not a failure.
import "std/testing"

type maybe err int

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

fn boom what
  err what

fn look _:maybe
  "the typeset arm took it"

fn look v
  "a value: {v}"

own = look (boom "a")

fine = look 7

pub play =
  print "own: {testing/when_failed own as_text}"
  >> print "value: {fine}"
